        {
            "author_association": "NONE",
            "body": "Thanks for your quick reply!\r\n\r\n> Thanks for the PR!\r\n> \r\n> By the quick looks of it, the current implementation is more safe, because:\r\n> \r\n>     * Supplying documentation as a table with only `kind` and no `value` does not follow the LSP specification. The value should be [`MarkupContent`](https://microsoft.github.io/language-server-protocol/specifications/lsp/3.17/specification/#markupContentInnerDefinition) which requires both fields present.\r\n> \r\n>     * The `documentation` (if present) [may also be string directly](https://microsoft.github.io/language-server-protocol/specifications/lsp/3.17/specification/#completionItem), which the change in the PR stops account for.\r\n\r\nAnd thanks for the refer, you're right, I'll report this issue to the kulala.nvim team.\r\n\r\n> \r\n> Could you please provide a minimal reproduction example so that I can see in more details on what is going on? My current understanding is that it is an issue with either LSP server or 'kulala.nvim'.\r\n\r\nHere's the minimal `init.lua` and reproduction step:\r\n```lua\r\n-- Put this at the top of 'init.lua'\r\nlocal path_package = vim.fn.getcwd() .. '/.nvim/site'\r\nlocal mini_path = path_package .. '/pack/deps/start/mini.nvim'\r\nif not vim.loop.fs_stat(mini_path) then\r\n  vim.cmd('echo \"Installing `mini.nvim`\" | redraw')\r\n  local clone_cmd = {\r\n    'git',\r\n    'clone',\r\n    '--filter=blob:none',\r\n    -- Uncomment next line to use 'stable' branch\r\n    -- '--branch', 'stable',\r\n    'https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim',\r\n    mini_path,\r\n  }\r\n  vim.fn.system(clone_cmd)\r\n  vim.cmd('packadd mini.nvim | helptags ALL')\r\nend\r\nrequire('mini.deps').setup({\r\n  path = {\r\n    package = path_package,\r\n  },\r\n})\r\nrequire('mini.completion').setup()\r\nlocal add = MiniDeps.add\r\n\r\nadd('mistweaverco/kulala.nvim')\r\nrequire('kulala').setup()\r\n```\r\n1. save it to some temporary dir\r\n2. switch to this dir\r\n3. launch neovim with this `init.lua` and edit a new http file with `nvim -u init.lua test.http`\r\n4. type something like `Au` to trigger mini.completion,  and then type `C-n` to see the error.\r\n\r\n> The following diff looks like a more appropriate solution:\r\n> \r\n> ```diff\r\n> diff --git a/lua/mini/completion.lua b/lua/mini/completion.lua\r\n> index 64e3db61..d0287256 100644\r\n> --- a/lua/mini/completion.lua\r\n> +++ b/lua/mini/completion.lua\r\n> @@ -1896,7 +1896,7 @@ H.normalize_item_doc = function(lsp_item, fallback_info)\r\n>  \r\n>    -- Extract string content. Treat markdown and plain kinds the same.\r\n>    -- Show both `detail` and `documentation` if the first provides new info.\r\n> -  detail, doc = detail or '', (type(doc) == 'table' and doc.value or doc) or ''\r\n> +  detail, doc = detail or '', type(doc) == 'table' and (doc.value or '') or doc\r\n>    -- Wrap details in language's code block to (usually) improve highlighting\r\n>    -- This approach seems to work in 'hrsh7th/nvim-cmp'\r\n>    detail = (H.is_whitespace(detail) or doc:find(detail, 1, true) ~= nil) and '' or (H.wrap_in_codeblock(detail) .. '\\n')\r\n> ```\r\n> \r\n> Does this solve the issue? Although I am pretty sure that LSP server would benefit from fixing this on their side.\r\n\r\nThis indeed solves the problem and also complies with the relevant LSP specifications. So are you planning to make these changes in mini.completion? I’m fine with whatever you decide, but I personally prefer making these changes. Some LSP implementations may not handle null and empty strings well, and  these changes many make the handling of `doc.value` more robust.",
